The '''Temple of the Kyber''',<ref name="UVG" /> also known as the '''Temple of the Whills'''<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> or simply the '''Kyber temple''',<ref name="UVG" /> was a great temple located in the [[Jedha City|Holy City]], a large, walled [[city]] on the<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> [[Temperature|cold]], [[desert]] [[moon]]<ref name="Jedha DB">{{Databank|jedha|Jedha}}</ref> of [[Jedha]]. The temple, rising high above the city wall, was held sacred by those who followed the teachings of the [[Church of the Force]].<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> The Temple was [[Destruction of Jedha City|destroyed]] when the [[Galactic Empire]] tested the first [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star's]] [[superlaser]] on the Holy City. Previously, the temple had been looted of the [[kyber crystal]]s it contained, and the [[Guardians of the Whills]], the temple's guardians, sent out into the street.<ref name="Rogue One">''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref>

==Description==
{{Quote|This temple's enormous spire stretched high above the walled Holy City of Jedha, welcoming all believers. The temple was home to the Church of the Force—a group whose followers had no Force-sensitivity yet still chose to follow the teachings of the Jedi Order. [...] The Temple of the Kyber was protected by the Guardians of the Whills—a near-extinct order of fiercely loyal warrior monks—until the Jedha's Holy City was obliterated when the Empire used the area as a test site for the first Death Star's superlaser.|[[Luke Skywalker]]|<ref name="Secrets of the Jedi">''[[Star Wars: The Secrets of the Jedi]]''</ref>}}
The Temple of the Kyber was located in the [[Jedha City|Holy City]] on [[Jedha]].<ref name="Galactic Atlas" /> There were tunnels that led to the temple underneath, which had [[kyber crystal]]s growing on the roof and in the ground. Lining the tunnels were 2,304 statues made entirely out of [[Kyber crystal|kyber]]. One statue in particular depicted a [[Jedi]] battling a serpent-like creature. The tunnels were guarded by the [[Guardians of the Whills|temple's guardians]], who protected the temple as their solemn vow.<ref name="Galaxy's Edge 3">[[Galaxy's Edge 3|''Galaxy's Edge'' 3]]</ref>

==History==
===High Republic Era===
[[File:TempleOfTheKyber-2022THR2.png|thumb|right|100px|The Temple of the Kyber during the [[High Republic Era]]]]
{{Quote|You have somewhere to stay? I work at the Temple of the Kyber. I could speak with one of the disciples&hellip;?|[[Keth Cerepath]]|<ref name="TENP">{{InsiderCite|issue1=213|issue2=214|Tales of Enlightenment: New Prospects}}</ref>}}
During the [[High Republic Era]], [[Keth Cerepath]] worked at the Temple of the Kyber. When the [[Prospector]] [[Saretha von Beel]] arrived on [[Jedha]] after a job gone wrong, he offered to ask the [[Disciples of the Whills]] if she could stay at the Temple, but she declined.<ref name="TENP" /> Around the same time, the [[Firebird of Colsassa]],<ref name="The High Republic 3" /> one of the 2,304 works of sacred art was stolen from the Temple of the Kyber.<ref name="THR2">[[The High Republic (2022) 2|''The High Republic'' (2022) 2]]</ref> Former [[Guardians of the Whills|Guardian of the Whills]]<ref name="The High Republic 3" /> [[Tey Sirrek]] and the two [[Jedi]] [[Vildar Mac]] and [[Matthea Cathley]] took it upon themselves to find the thief. They visited the Temple of the Kyber, but when they arrived the Guardians pointed their [[Lightbow]]s at them,<ref name="THR2" /> because Sirrek had once betrayed the Guardians.<ref name="The High Republic 3" />

===Dok-Ondar's Heist===
[[File:KyberJediStatue-GalaxysEdge3.png|thumb|left|150px|One of the 2,304 statues that lined the tunnels inside the Temple of the Kyber]]
During the [[Imperial Era|early Imperial Era]], [[Dok-Ondar]] along with [[Hondo Ohnaka]] and [[Quadoro]] successfully managed to [[Heist at the Temple of the Kyber|steal]] one of the [[Unidentified statue (Jedha)|statues]] lining the tunnels underneath the temple. Dok-Ondar stated that the statue he took must've been at least 2000 years old, being able to tell from the growth of [[Kyber crystal|kyber]] at the base. He also stated that the statue must've been carved by a Jedi with their lightsaber. The group faced many [[Guardians of the Whills|guardians]] of the temple when entering to retrieve the relic, however they all were defeated by Hondo and his blaster. Only when leaving did the group face [[Chirrut Îmwe]], who was able to both dodge Hondo's blaster bolts and disarm him. Dok-Ondar was able to reason with Îmwe, stating that the statue was only one of 2,304 statues that lined the tunnels underneath the temple. Dok-Ondar also stated that the statue was of specific importance to him, as he inherited one of the same statues from his parents when they died. After hearing Dok-Ondar's reasoning, Îmwe allowed him and Hondo to leave with the statue.<ref name="Galaxy's Edge 3" />

==Behind the scenes==
The Whills first appeared in the original drafts of [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''A New Hope'']] as an equivalent name for [[the Force]].<ref name="annotated">''[[Star Wars: The Annotated Screenplays]]''</ref> The term appeared in a smattering of [[Star Wars Legends|Legends]] sources, such as the [[Star Wars: A New Hope (novelization)|''A New Hope'' novelization]], and was made [[canon]] in [[2015]] when the [[Star Wars: The Force Awakens (novelization)|novelization]] of the film [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']] referred to the [[Journal of the Whills]].

Early concept art for ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'' used the [[Temple of Pomojema]] as a setting, before the Temple of the Kyber was developed to replace it.<ref name="ArtR1">''[[The Art of Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref>

